// mlrepro (2026-07-16, v-compiler-ml, dogfooding the Cadenza compiler's own lazy query-DB): a TWO-operation
// effect handler whose STATE is a heap value (a `Map`) — one op READS the state (`get` → `Map.lookup`
// returned as an `Option`) and the other WRITES it (`put` → `Map.insert`) — DECLINES:
//   "this handler is not yet reducible by the tail-resumptive fold (cross-function or non-tail resume
//    arrives in a later increment)"
//
// This is EXACTLY the shape a memoized query-DB needs (the salsa-style `Db` the operator asked the Cadenza
// compiler to mirror from rcdzc): `get(key) -> Option value` + `put(key, value) -> Unit` over a threaded
// `Map` store. So the effect-based Db substrate (design (A)) is currently blocked; the port falls back to a
// pure threaded-Db (design (B), which compiles + runs fine) — but the effect form is the truest mirror of
// rcdzc's `&mut Db`, so this decline is the load-bearing friction to lift.
//
// ISOLATED — the trigger is the COMBINATION (each of these alone COMPILES + runs):
//   - a single-op handler with `Map` state (read + insert in the arm)  ✅ works
//   - a TWO-op handler with `Int64` state (`g`/`p`)                     ✅ works
//   - a TWO-op handler with `Map` state where `get` returns a lookup    ✗ DECLINES  ← this file
// So neither "two ops" nor "Map state" alone is the wall; it's two ops + heap-typed state + a get that
// returns a value read from that state. (Contrast: `Fresh`-style Int64 state, even cross-function, folds.)
//
// EXPECTED: the two-op Map-state handler folds like the Int64 one — `run()` below returns 42.

effect Db =
  | get : Int64 -> Option(Int64)
  | put : Tuple(Int64, Int64) -> Unit

def run() =
  handle Db(Map.empty) with
    | get(k, s) => resume(Map.lookup(s, k), s)
    | put(kv, s) => (match kv with | (k, v) => resume(unit, Map.insert(s, k, v)))
  in
  (Db.put((1, 42)); match Db.get(1) with | Option.Some(v) => v | Option.None(_) => 0 - 1)

@test
def two-op-map-state-handler() =
  if run() == 42 then unit else trap("put 1→42 then get 1 should be 42")

export { run }
